course: dictionary
lesson: 7633
title: Prodigal: spending freely and coming home
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prodigal means spending money or things too freely and wastefully. You will meet it in the Bible story of the prodigal son, a young man who wastes his father's money. «In the Prodigal Son a younger son wastes his share of his father's money and comes home ashamed.» The clue is in the wasting, and the story is about coming home.
explain: The story also shows a second meaning. «The prodigal son came home after a long time, and his father ran to meet him.» When a word describes a person who goes away and comes back, it can be a noun, so a prodigal is someone who returns after a long absence.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The wasteful son came home after a long time» still makes sense, so prodigal means wasteful here. Everyday people joke, «the prodigal returns», when someone walks in late after being away.
fact: Prodigal can be an adjective (a prodigal son) or a noun (a prodigal, prodigals).
fact: «Prodigal» means spending money or things too freely and wastefully.
fact: «The prodigal son» is a Bible story about a young man who wastes his father's money, comes home sorry, and is welcomed with love.
fact: «Prodigal with money» means spending it too freely, without thinking about tomorrow.
fact: In everyday speech, «the prodigal returns» is a joke about someone coming home after a long time away.
exercise: Lily says, «She is prodigal with her pocket money; it's gone by Monday.» What does prodigal suggest? | Saves every penny | Counts her change carefully | Gives none of it away | *Spends too much, too freely
exercise: Dad says «the prodigal returns!» when Sam walks in after a week at Grandma's. What does it mean? | *Someone who comes back home after a long time away | A loud, angry shout | A cook who makes dinner | A pet that runs off
exercise: In the Bible story, why is the father happy when the prodigal son comes home? | He is angry and sends him away | He has forgotten his son | *He has missed his son and welcomes him with love | He wants the pigs
test: «Ella was prodigal with her birthday money and had none left by the weekend.» What does prodigal mean here? | Saving it very carefully | *Spending it too freely | Sharing it fairly with her friends | Hiding it under her bed
test: In the phrase «a prodigal», what kind of word is it? | *A noun for someone who comes home after a long time away | An adverb for slowly | A word for a loud noise | A verb for running fast
exam: Which sentence uses prodigal correctly? | *He was prodigal with his savings and bought ten toys in one afternoon | She counted every coin before she spent it | The swimmers were prodigal and took turns to rest | He saved his birthday money in a jar
